steps.body:
`setTimeout(callback, ms)` asks Node to run `callback` once, at least `ms` milliseconds later. It returns right away, so the rest of your code keeps going.

```javascript
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("later");
}, 20);
console.log("now");
```

This prints `now`, then `later`. JavaScript runs one thing at a time: all synchronous code finishes first, and only then does the **event loop** run waiting callbacks. Even `setTimeout(fn, 0)` waits for the current code to finish.

Timers fire in order of their delays, not in the order you created them. Keep delays clearly apart (10 ms or more) when their order matters, since a timer is a minimum wait, not an exact one. The program does not exit at its last line: Node keeps running until the last scheduled callback has run.

Gotcha: a callback reads its variables when it runs, not when it was scheduled. Declare loop variables with `const` or `let` so each timer keeps its own value.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const timers = input.trim().split("\n").map((line) => {
  const [label, delayText] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  return { label, delay: Number(delayText) };
});

console.log("start");
for (const { label, delay } of timers) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(`${label} after ${delay} ms`);
  }, delay);
}
console.log(`scheduled ${timers.length} timers`);

